    "reply": "The retrieved records cover only two parts of this question: BRCA1 missense variants in ClinVar and a few hereditary cancer conditions. They do not include the BRCA1 gene record, clinical significance calls for the variants, genetic tests, or literature.\n\n## Associated Conditions\n\nThe graph links BRCA1-related records to Hereditary breast ovarian cancer syndrome [2]. It also names Hereditary cancer-predisposing syndrome [4]. A third linked condition is Familial breast-ovarian cancer susceptibility 1 [12].\n\n## ClinVar Variants\n\nThe variants returned are all single-nucleotide missense changes described on the NM_007294.4 transcript. One is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4709T>C (p.Leu1570Pro) [1]. Another is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.353T>C (p.Leu118Pro) [5]. A third is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4685C>G (p.Pro1562Arg) [15]. A fourth is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4652C>T (p.Ser1551Phe) [17]. A fifth is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4586T>G (p.Ise1529Ser) [27].\n\n## Coverage Across the Protein\n\nThe variants span much of the coding sequence, from the N-terminal region to the C-terminal region. An N-terminal example is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.500C>T (p.Thr167Ile) [24]. A central example is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.3664G>A (p.Glu1222Lys) [3].\n\n## Gaps in Coverage\n\nThe records do not state a clinical significance for any variant, so none can be called pathogenic from these data. They also contain no gene record, genetic test listings, or PubMed articles.",
